 reductase. It is a metabolite that can accumulate in the lens of the eye, particularly in patients with galactosemia, a metabolic disorder characterized by the inability to properly metabolize galactose. This accumulation of galactitol is associated with the development of cataracts, a common complication in individuals with classic galactosemia and those with galactokinase deficiency. In patients with galactosemia, monitoring the levels of galactitol is important to assess dietary compliance and potential transgressions, as elevated levels can indicate toxicity and contribute to the risk of cataract formation.\",6\r\n9497,GALACTOSE,\"Galactose is a monosaccharide sugar that plays a significant role in various metabolic processes. It is a component of lactose, the sugar found in milk, and is also part of galactolipids, which are important for cellular structures. In the context of dietary considerations, galactose is permitted in the diets of patients with certain glycogen storage diseases, indicating its relevance in specific health conditions. Overall, galactose is metabolized by the body and is involved in multiple metabolic pathways, highlighting its importance in human physiology.\",5\r\n9563,ERITROCITOS,\"Erythrocytes, also known as red blood cells, play a significant role in the diagnosis of galactosemia by measuring GALT activity and galactose levels. These cells express the galactokinase gene (GALK1), which is crucial for galactose metabolism. Additionally, erythrocytes exhibit enzymatic activity related to galactose, further highlighting their importance in metabolic processes involving this sugar.\",4\r\n9587,GAL-1-P,\"Gal-1-P, also known as Galactose-1-phosphate, is a significant metabolite measured in patients to monitor dietary compliance and metabolic control in individuals with galactosemia. This substance is particularly elevated in patients with certain forms of galactosemia, making it a crucial marker in the screening and management of the disorder.\",3\r\n9511,AMNIOCENTESIS,\"Amniocentesis is a medical procedure utilized for prenatal diagnosis, allowing for the analysis of amniotic fluid to detect various genetic conditions. This procedure can be employed to identify galactosemia, a metabolic disorder, as well as to detect the mutation associated with Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (GSDVI). Through the examination of the amniotic fluid, healthcare providers can gain valuable insights into the genetic health of the fetus.\",2\r\n9562,GALT,\"GALT, or Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase, is a crucial enzyme responsible for the metabolism of galactose. The gene GALT is essential for this process, and mutations in this gene lead to a condition known as classical galactosemia. The activity of the GALT enzyme is measured to diagnose galactosemia and its various forms. This enzyme plays a significant role in the proper processing of galactose, and its deficiency can result in serious metabolic complications.\",2\r\n9569,NIVEL DE GAL-1-P,\"NIVEL DE GAL-1-P is a significant marker in the diagnosis and management of classic galactosemia. The levels of Gal-1-P in erythrocytes serve as an important indicator for diagnosing this condition. Additionally, monitoring the level of Gal-1-P is crucial for assessing the effectiveness of dietary restrictions in individuals affected by galactosemia.\",2\r\n9595,GALE,\"GALE is a gene located on chromosome 1p36 that plays a crucial role in the metabolism of galactose. It encodes an enzyme known as uridin difosfato galactosa-4-epimerasa (GALE), which is essential for the proper processing of galactose in the body. Mutations or deficiencies in the GALE gene can lead to various clinical deficits, highlighting its importance in metabolic pathways related to galactose.\",2\r\n9594,GALK1,\"GALK1 is the gene that encodes the enzyme galactokinase, located on chromosome 17q25.1.\",4\r\n9581,AFRICANS,\"Africans, particularly those native to South Africa, are also frequently affected by the most common clinical variant of galactosemia, sharing the same genetic mutation as Afroamericans.\",1\r\n9580,AFROAMERICANS,\"Afroamericans are a demographic group that is frequently affected by the most common clinical variant of galactosemia, which is associated with a specific genetic mutation.\",1\r\n9593,ALIMENTOS MANUFACTURADOS,Manufactured foods may contain galactose or lactose and should be carefully checked by individuals with galactosemia.,1\r\n9591,ALIMENTOS PROHIBIDOS,\"Foods that are prohibited for individuals with galactosemia, which contain more than 20 mg of galactose per 100 g.\",1\r\n9584,ALÉLO DUARTE,\"The Duarte allele is a genetic variant associated with a milder form of galactosemia, affecting GALT activity in individuals.\",1\r\n9586,BIOPSIA CORIAL,\"Chorionic villus sampling (biopsia corial) is a prenatal test that can be used to diagnose genetic disorders, including galactosemia.\",1\r\n9582,DUARTE VARIANT,\"The Duarte variant is a biochemical variant of galactosemia characterized by partial residual GALT activity, leading to milder symptoms and complications compared to classical galactosemia.\",1\r\n9602,DÉFICIT GENERALIZADO,\"Déficit generalizado is a clinical form of galactosemia where GALE activity is deficient in all tissues, resembling classic galactosemia.\",1\r\n9603,DÉFICIT INTERMEDIO,\"Déficit intermedio is a clinical form of galactosemia with reduced GALE activity in erythrocytes and leukocytes, but not presenting acute symptoms at birth.\",1\r\n9601,DÉFICIT PERIFÉRICO,\"Déficit periférico is a clinical form of galactosemia limited to erythrocytes and leukocytes, resulting in asymptomatic individuals.\",1\r\n9589,FORMULAS DE SOJA,Soy formulas are recommended for infants with galactosemia as a substitute for milk-based formulas.,1\r\n9578,GALACTOLÍPIDOS,\"Galactolípidos are lipids that contain galactose, playing a significant role in the context of metabolic disorders associated with galactose. Additionally, they are essenti